  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe current approach relies on a long sequence of optimization phases with multiple interpolation and projection steps. This is a valid method for a challenging nonconvex optimization problem, but the computation time is dominated by the gradient evaluations (auto-differentiation) and repeated constraint enforcement. The key bottlenecks are:\n- The `compute_erdos` function fires an unoptimized gradient (Central Difference) for nearly every step\n- The Nelder-Mead and DE solvers don't scale well to large problems\n- The correlation computation is inherently O(n^3) through auto-diff with jitter\n\nA fundamentally faster approach: use direct gradient computation in precompiled kernels (via just-in-time compilation) and reformulate the problem with separable components. We'll keep the core \"sparse grid\" idea but use convolution to compute the gradient without autodiff, and parallelize gradient computations. This parallel gradient descent using fixed-points.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\na) Objective: min max_k \u222b[0,2] h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\nb) Constraints:\n   \u2022 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n   \u2022 \u222b[0,2] h(x) dx = 1\nc) Discretization: h(x_i) for i=1..n (n point samples over [0,2])\nd) Optimization variables: h \u2208 R^n with bounds [0,1]\ne) Feasible functions: sum(h) * dx = desired_target = n/2\nf) Gradient for variable i: \n   \u2202/\u2202h_i [h(x_j)(1-h(x_j+k))] for all k\n   = (if j=i) h_i * (if k current lag -1 else -h_i+k) + (if i a}=j+k) h_j * (1-')\n\ng) Instead of an exact global optimizer, we use:\n   - Coordinate-wise updates (local Nelder-Mead)\n   - Precomputed gradient vector using direct formula\n   - Set fixed-point updates between optimization phases\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na) Precomputation:\n   \u2022 n = len(initial_h_values)\n   \u2022 dx = 2.0/n\n   \u2022 desired_target = n/2 * dx\n\nb) Core gradient function:\n   ```python\n   def gradient_function(h):\n       h = np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n       corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n       # Return gradient of cdot_wrt_h: much simpler using index-wise derivatives\n       # Differentiation with respect to h_i:\n       #   \u2202(overlap at k with reference index i) = \n       #     (if current index i) [h_i * (1-h_i+k) + h_j * (-1)] where j = i+k or j = i-k mod n\n       # But let's compute:\n       n = len(h)\n       grad = np.zeros(n)\n       # For each point i, consider all possible shifts where i appears\n       for jitter in range(2):  # Fixed jitters to add stability\n         # Apply boundary placement with jitter\n         pass  # details omitted for brevity but would compute gradient using explicit index formulas\n       return grad * (some scaling factor)\n   ```\n\nc) Optimization strategy:\n   \u2022 Use fixed-point nonlocal search:\n     while time_est < 1300 and improvement:\n        - Run hillclimb with:\n          x_next = x_best + step_size\n          enforce sum(x) == desired_target\n          enforce 0\u2264x\u22641\n        - Compute objective without gradient\n   \u2022 Local refinement:\n     while time_remaining > 100s:\n        - Use coordinate-wise NLopt algorithm on subsets\n        - Calculate exact gradients\n        - Only update during directions where improvement > 1e-6\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\na) Initial config:\n   \u2022 Population size=500\n   \u2022 Initial step_size=0.5/dx\n   \u2022 Parsimony pressure=0.1\n   \u2022 Mutation rate=0.2\n   \u2022 use_convolution_sampling=True\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\na) Time estimation:\n   \u2022 70% for global search\n   \u2022 20% for gradient descent\n   \u2022 10% for local refinements\n\n6. Validation and return contract\na) Return the best found h with:\n   \u2022 sum(h) * dx \u2248 desired_target\n   \u2022 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n   \u2022 Objective value computed with clip to 0/1\n   \u2022 Final candidate re-evaluated with exact formula",
